  "body": "Hey!\r\n\r\nCurrently, the standard says that any `<link>` element with an `href` attribute should be loaded. This also applies to alternate stylesheets (unlikely to be used) and stylesheets with a non-matching media query, even when it's unlikely to change (`screen` vs `print` or `prefers-color-scheme`). For example, if one wants to provide a stylesheet for light mode and another for dark mode, both stylesheets will be loaded, wasting bandwidth. Due to this, there is little gain to provide separate stylesheets for different usage as everything is loaded. While a user may dynamically change from dark mode to light mode, it is a rare change and loading the appropriate stylesheet on first use is seems a low inconvenience compared to always downloading two stylesheets and using only one.\r\n\r\nA stylesheet could be marked with a boolean `optional` that would prevent loading it when it is an alternate stylesheet or when the media query does not match. This will be the responsibility of the document author to decide when a stylesheet is optional or not. This way, we do not need to be able to make a difference between two media queries (for example, a media query using the device width and a media query for color scheme).",
